<!DOCTYPE html>
<html class="brand-color-background antialiased" lang="en" data-theme="light" style="font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />

    <!-- Primary Meta Tags -->
    <title>Free Readability Checker - Test Reading Level and Grade - Coffee &amp; Fun LLC</title>
    <meta name="title" content="Free Readability Checker - Test Reading Level and Grade - Coffee &amp; Fun LLC" />
    <meta name="description" content="Paste your writing and see its reading level in plain English, with Flesch Reading Ease, Flesch Kincaid grade, Gunning Fog, SMOG, Coleman Liau and ARI scores, plus the exact sentences and words to fix. Free, no sign up, runs in your browser." />
    <meta name="author" content="Coffee &amp; Fun LLC" />
    <meta name="robots" content="index, follow" />

    <!-- Canonical URL -->
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/" />

    <!-- Favicon -->
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/images/brand/favicon.png" sizes="any" />

    <!-- Open Graph -->
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Coffee &amp; Fun" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Free Readability Checker - Test Reading Level and Grade" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="See your writing's reading level in plain English, and the exact sentences and words worth fixing. Free and private, nothing leaves your browser." />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/assets/images/social/pages/readability-checker.png" />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content="Readability Checker card showing a reading level gauge and the Coffee and Fun cup logo" />
    <meta property="og:locale" content="en_US" />

    <!-- Twitter Card -->
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:site" content="@bycoffeeandfun" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="Free Readability Checker - Test Reading Level and Grade" />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="See your writing's reading level in plain English, and the exact sentences and words worth fixing. Free and private, nothing leaves your browser." />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://www.coffeeandfun.com/assets/images/social/pages/readability-checker.png" />
    <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="Readability Checker card showing a reading level gauge and the Coffee and Fun cup logo" />

    <meta name="theme-color" content="#fef5ec" />

    <!-- Structured Data -->
    <script type="application/ld+json">
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@graph": [
          {
            "@type": "WebApplication",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/#app",
            "name": "Readability Checker",
            "alternateName": "Reading Level Test",
            "description": "Check the reading level of any text with six readability formulas, and see which sentences and words are making it harder to read.",
            "url": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/",
            "applicationCategory": "UtilitiesApplication",
            "operatingSystem": "Web Browser",
            "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
            "featureList": [
              "Flesch Reading Ease score",
              "Flesch Kincaid grade level",
              "Gunning Fog index",
              "SMOG index",
              "Coleman Liau index",
              "Automated Readability Index",
              "Plain English reading level verdict",
              "Longest sentences listed for rewriting",
              "Complex word list with syllable counts",
              "Word, sentence, syllable and reading time counts",
              "Runs entirely in the browser with no uploads"
            ],
            "isAccessibleForFree": true,
            "inLanguage": "en",
            "publisher": { "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/#organization" },
            "creator": { "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/#founder" },
            "isPartOf": { "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/#website" }
          },
          {
            "@type": "FAQPage",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/#faq",
            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What reading level should I aim for?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "For a general audience, grade 8 to 10 is a good target. News writing usually lands around grade 9. Government and health information is often written to grade 6 to 8 so it reaches as many people as possible. Technical documentation for specialists can sit higher without being a problem."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is a good Flesch Reading Ease score?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Flesch Reading Ease runs from 0 to 100, and higher means easier. Scores of 60 to 70 suit a general adult audience. Below 30 the text is genuinely hard and most readers will struggle. Above 80 is very easy and suits younger readers or anyone skim reading."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Why does the SMOG index say it needs 30 sentences?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The SMOG formula was designed and validated on samples of 30 sentences. Run it on less than that and the result swings wildly, so this tool holds it back rather than showing a number you cannot trust. Every other formula still works on shorter text."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does my text get uploaded anywhere?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No. Every calculation happens in your browser using JavaScript on this page. Your text is never sent to a server, never stored, and never logged. You can disconnect from the internet after the page loads and the tool still works."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Why do the six formulas disagree with each other?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Each formula measures something slightly different. Flesch Kincaid and Gunning Fog count syllables, Coleman Liau and ARI count letters, and SMOG counts long words. Text full of short technical terms scores differently from text full of long plain words. The consensus grade averages them so one formula's blind spot does not decide the result."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does this work for languages other than English?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No. These formulas were built and validated on English, and the syllable counting rules are English rules. Running other languages through them produces numbers, but those numbers do not mean anything."
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
            "@id": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/#breadcrumbs",
            "itemListElement": [
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/" },
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Apps and tools", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/apps/" },
              { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Readability Checker", "item": "https://www.coffeeandfun.com/readability-checker/" }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    </script>

    <script src="https://unpkg.com/vue@3"></script>

    {% bundledCss %}
    {% pageCss "readability-checker" %}
    {% include sketch-styles.liquid %}
    {% include schema-org.liquid %}
</head>

<body>
    {% include header.liquid %}
{% raw %}
    <a href="#tool" class="skip">Skip to the checker</a>

    <main v-cloak id="main-content" class="wrap">

        <header id="tool" class="page-head">
            <h1 class="brand-heading">Readability checker</h1>
            <p class="lede">
                Paste your writing below and see how hard it is to read, in plain English rather than
                jargon. You get a reading level, six standard readability scores, and the exact
                sentences and words worth changing. Nothing you type leaves your browser.
            </p>
        </header>

        <!-- Live status for screen readers -->
        <div class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite">{{ announcement }}</div>

        <!-- ===== 1: the text ===== -->
        <section class="cf-step" aria-labelledby="h-text">
            <h2 class="step-label" id="h-text">1. Your text</h2>

            <label class="sr-only" for="rc-input">The text you want to check</label>
            <textarea id="rc-input" ref="input" class="msg" v-model="text"
                      placeholder="Paste or type your writing here…"
                      aria-describedby="rc-stats" spellcheck="true"></textarea>

            <div class="io-row">
                <span class="counter" id="rc-stats">{{ statsLine }}</span>
                <span class="io-actions">
                    <button v-if="text" type="button" class="linkish" @click="clearText">Clear</button>
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="samples">
                <span class="samples-label" id="h-samples">Or try an example:</span>
                <span class="join" role="group" aria-labelledby="h-samples">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm join-item" @click="loadSample('simple')">Simple</button>
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm join-item" @click="loadSample('average')">Average</button>
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm join-item" @click="loadSample('complex')">Complex</button>
                </span>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ===== 2: the verdict ===== -->
        <section class="cf-step" aria-labelledby="h-result">
            <h2 class="step-label" id="h-result">2. How it reads</h2>

            <div v-if="!hasEnough" class="empty-state">
                <p>Add at least ten words and your reading level will appear here as you type.</p>
            </div>

            <template v-else>
                <div class="verdict" :class="'band-' + band.label.toLowerCase().replace(/ /g, '-')">
                    <p class="verdict-line">
                        Reads like <strong>{{ verdict.grade }}</strong>
                    </p>
                    <p class="verdict-band">{{ verdict.label }}</p>
                    <p class="verdict-who">{{ verdict.who }}.</p>
                </div>

                <ul class="quickstats">
                    <li><span class="qs-num">{{ wordCount }}</span> <span class="qs-lab">words</span></li>
                    <li><span class="qs-num">{{ sentenceCount }}</span> <span class="qs-lab">sentences</span></li>
                    <li><span class="qs-num">{{ readingTime }}</span> <span class="qs-lab">to read</span></li>
                    <li><span class="qs-num">{{ complexPercent }}%</span> <span class="qs-lab">long words</span></li>
                </ul>
            </template>
        </section>

        <!-- ===== 3: what to change ===== -->
        <section v-if="hasEnough" class="cf-step" aria-labelledby="h-fix">
            <h2 class="step-label" id="h-fix">3. What to change</h2>

            <div v-for="s in suggestions" :key="s.id" class="suggestion">
                <h3 class="sugg-title">{{ s.title }}</h3>
                <p class="sugg-detail">{{ s.detail }}</p>
            </div>

            <div v-if="longSentences.length" class="fixlist">
                <h3 class="fixlist-title">
                    Your longest sentences
                    <span class="fixlist-count">{{ longSentences.length }} over 25 words</span>
                </h3>
                <ol class="sentence-list">
                    <li v-for="(s, i) in longSentences" :key="i">
                        <span class="sentence-len">{{ s.words }} words</span>
                        <span class="sentence-text">{{ s.text }}</span>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div v-if="complexWords.length" class="fixlist">
                <h3 class="fixlist-title">
                    Longest words
                    <span class="fixlist-count">{{ complexWords.length }} with 3+ syllables</span>
                </h3>
                <ul class="word-list">
                    <li v-for="w in complexWords.slice(0, 24)" :key="w.word">
                        <span class="wl-word">{{ w.word }}</span>
                        <span class="wl-syll">{{ w.syllables }} syllables</span>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ===== 4: the formulas ===== -->
        <section v-if="hasEnough" class="cf-step" aria-labelledby="h-scores">
            <h2 class="step-label" id="h-scores">4. The six scores</h2>
            <p class="section-note">
                Each formula measures something slightly different. The reading level above is the
                average of the ones that apply to your text.
            </p>

            <div class="formula-grid">
                <div v-for="f in formulaRows" :key="f.key" class="formula">
                    <p class="formula-name">{{ f.name }}</p>
                    <p v-if="!f.unavailable" class="formula-value">
                        {{ f.value }}<span class="formula-unit" v-if="f.unit === 'grade'"> grade</span>
                    </p>
                    <p v-else class="formula-value formula-na">Not yet</p>
                    <p class="formula-plain">{{ f.why || f.plain }}</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="result-actions">
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-lg" style="--btn-color: var(--color-cocoa); --btn-fg: #fff" @click="copySummary">
                    {{ copied ? 'Copied ✓' : 'Copy the summary' }}
                </button>
            </div>
        </section>
{% endraw %}

        <!-- ===== Reference and FAQ: real content, and what this page ranks for ===== -->
        <section class="cf-step" aria-labelledby="h-guide">
            <h2 class="step-label" id="h-guide">What the grade levels mean</h2>
            <p class="section-note">
                Readability formulas report a US school grade. It is a rough guide to how much effort
                a reader has to spend, not a judgement of how good the writing is.
            </p>

            <div class="table-scroll">
                <table class="band-table">
                    <caption class="sr-only">Reading grade levels and the audiences they suit</caption>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">Grade</th>
                            <th scope="col">Difficulty</th>
                            <th scope="col">Who reads it comfortably</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th scope="row">1 to 5</th><td>Very easy</td><td>Almost everyone, including younger readers</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">6 to 8</th><td>Easy</td><td>Most adults read this without effort</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">9 to 10</th><td>Fairly easy</td><td>Comfortable for a general audience</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">11 to 12</th><td>Moderate</td><td>Fine for most adults who are paying attention</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">13 to 14</th><td>Hard</td><td>Assumes a confident, motivated reader</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">15 to 17</th><td>Very hard</td><td>Degree level. Many readers will give up</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">18 and up</th><td>Extremely hard</td><td>Specialist or academic readers only</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cf-step" aria-labelledby="h-faq">
            <h2 class="step-label" id="h-faq">Common questions</h2>

            <div class="faq">
                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">What reading level should I aim for?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>For a general audience, grade 8 to 10 is a good target. News writing usually lands
                        around grade 9. Government and health information is often written to grade 6 to 8 so
                        it reaches as many people as possible. Technical documentation for specialists can sit
                        higher without being a problem.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">What is a good Flesch Reading Ease score?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>Flesch Reading Ease runs from 0 to 100, and higher means easier. Scores of 60 to 70
                        suit a general adult audience. Below 30 the text is genuinely hard and most readers
                        will struggle. Above 80 is very easy and suits younger readers or anyone skim reading.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">Why does the SMOG index say it needs 30 sentences?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>The SMOG formula was designed and validated on samples of 30 sentences. Run it on
                        less than that and the result swings wildly, so this tool holds it back rather than
                        showing a number you cannot trust. Every other formula still works on shorter text.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">Does my text get uploaded anywhere?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>No. Every calculation happens in your browser using JavaScript on this page. Your
                        text is never sent to a server, never stored, and never logged. You can disconnect
                        from the internet after the page loads and the tool still works.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">Why do the six formulas disagree with each other?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>Each formula measures something slightly different. Flesch Kincaid and Gunning Fog
                        count syllables, Coleman Liau and ARI count letters, and SMOG counts long words. Text
                        full of short technical terms scores differently from text full of long plain words.
                        The consensus grade averages them so one formula's blind spot does not decide the result.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="collapse collapse-arrow faq-item">
                    <summary class="collapse-title">Does this work for languages other than English?</summary>
                    <div class="collapse-content">
                        <p>No. These formulas were built and validated on English, and the syllable counting
                        rules are English rules. Running other languages through them produces numbers, but
                        those numbers do not mean anything.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>
        </section>

        <p class="foot">Everything is worked out on your device. Nothing you paste here is uploaded, stored or logged.</p>
    </main>

    {% include modals.liquid %}
    {% include footer.liquid %}

    {% pageJs "readability-checker" %}
</body>
</html>
